# IMFEED INVESTIGATION ITEM — w-belt-cube-mechanism

## Source findings (4 models, all lanes)
- All 4 models agree: no verified build for belt-driven orthogonal cube with 3 GT2 loops + corner pinions
- Hermes identified the crux: GT2 teeth are curved (not involute); a printed pinion meshing belt on 2 orthogonal faces at once has no published confirmation
- OWED: tooth profile under side load, corner derailment vs stall tension, phase-lock tuning

## Proposed investigation
{
  "ask": "What is the exact GT2 timing-belt tooth geometry — pitch (2mm), tooth height (4mm), root radius, crest radius, pressure angle, and the curved-profile equation — and has anyone published a 3D-printed pinion or gear that successfully meshes with GT2 belt teeth, especially in a corner configuration where the belt wraps on two orthogonal faces simultaneously? Search: GT2 belt tooth profile specification, 3D printed GT2 pinion mesh, OpenBuilds MartinMueller pulley profile geometry, belt-driven cubic mechanism.",
  "tier": "free",
  "max_tokens": 3000
}

## Rationale
- Deepest branch: the corner pinion geometry is the mechanical gate. Everything else (torque, tension, phase-lock) depends on it.
- Adjacent unknown: the GT2 tooth profile is a published standard — searchable. Printed-gear mesh evidence may exist in RepRap/Thingiverse communities.
- Not a repeat: models confirmed no source exists but did not retrieve the GT2 spec itself or search for printed-pinion precedents.

## Acceptance criteria
- GT2 tooth profile parameters found (manufacturer datasheet or ISO standard reference)
- At least one published 3D-printed pinion/gear confirmed meshing with GT2 belt (Thingiverse, GitHub, academic)
- If orthogonal corner case found: note geometry, tension, and failure mode
- If none found: state the gap explicitly so the next model knows what remains OWED
